\oscserver creates an OSC server, to receive OSC messages.
Its syntax is the following:

\oscserver myserver

The name can't be the name of a LiveLily command.
Once created, the server is used like a command with its
name, and its methods are concatenated with a dot.

The setup method sets the port to listen to. It must be
1024 or higher:

\myserver.setup 5010

If the port is used by another program, you will get an
error.

The map method maps an OSC address to a function. The
address must be inside double quotes and start with a /,
and the function must already be defined:

\function setvar {
    \print $1
}

\myserver.map "/foo" setvar

Every message received at /foo calls \setvar, with the
arguments of the message as the function's arguments
($1, $2, etc.).
Mapping the same address again replaces its function.
